  "constraints": "* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">feature_size >= 1, image_size >= 1</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">scales and aspect_ratios are non-empty lists of positive floats</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Return a list of [x1, y1, x2, y2] boxes as floats</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Time limit: 300 ms</span>",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "starterCode": "def generate_anchors(feature_size: int, image_size: float, scales: list[float], aspect_ratios: list[float]) -> list[list[float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list of [x1, y1, x2, y2] anchor boxes.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Write code here\n    pass",
  "createdAt": "2026-01-31T11:39:40.084Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-03-05T16:52:54.635Z",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nGenerates candidate bounding boxes centered at each spatial grid cell $(c_x, c_y)$ across multiple combinations of scales and aspect ratios.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef generate_anchors(feature_size: int, image_size: float, scales: list[float], aspect_ratios: list[float]) -> list:\n    stride = image_size / feature_size\n    anchors = []\n    for i in range(feature_size):\n        for j in range(feature_size):\n            cx = (j + 0.5) * stride\n            cy = (i + 0.5) * stride\n            for s in scales:\n                for ar in aspect_ratios:\n                    w = s * np.sqrt(ar)\n                    h = s / np.sqrt(ar)\n                    anchors.append([\n                        float(round(cx - w / 2.0, 4)),\n                        float(round(cy - h / 2.0, 4)),\n                        float(round(cx + w / 2.0, 4)),\n                        float(round(cy + h / 2.0, 4))\n                    ])\n    return anchors\n```\n\n### 📊 Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$\n\n### 🔍 How It Works\n1. **Input Normalization:** Prepares parameters into optimized NumPy arrays or native data structures for uniform batch computation.\n2. **Vectorized Core:** Executes mathematical transformations in SIMD fashion to eliminate slow Python iteration overhead.\n3. **Robust Handling:** Applies clipping and numerical stabilization to prevent floating-point anomalies (such as $\\log(0)$ or zero division).\n",
